  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak ridge of high pressure will start to build in overnight \nwith much improved conditions for today, decreasing clouds \nacross much of the area, except for possibly the NYS Thruway \ncorridor on northward, where clouds from lake effect moisture\nmay persist. Highs are expected to warm up in the upper 50s to \nlow 60s for most of the region. Wednesday should remain mostly \ndry, but an incoming clipper system could start spreading rain \nshowers into the region by Wednesday evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe pattern will become more active for the end of the week and\ninto the weekend. We're seeing agreement in model guidance that\nshows large upper trough settles over the Northeast U.S. \nThursday into Friday. However, some model guidance tries to \ndevelop low pressure off the southern coast of New England or \nLong Island, with some outliers having the center low off the \ncoast more. There remains quite a bit of spread on where/if this\nsystem will develop and track, as timing and strength remains \ninconsistent. What does appear more likely at this time is our \nregion will be at least under the influence of an upper level \ntrough/low and cooler temperatures are likely for the end of the\nweek and this weekend. As for potential snowfall, boundary \nlayer temperatures at this time appear fairly marginal, with the\nbest chance for any snow accumulations to occur during the \novernight hours Friday into Saturday and most likely in the \nhigher elevation areas. \n\nThis upper level low could persist through the weekend and into\nthe first part of next week. This would keep conditions cool and\nunsettled under northwest flow, with lake effect rain/snow \nshowers possible.",
